// contractio -- §5.4's three reduction shapes over ONE input, from source.
//
// WHAT THIS PROGRAM IS FOR. §14 entry 28's claim is that a declared
// reduction shape is OBSERVABLE: the same twenty `f32` values, summed
// under `forma ordinata`, `forma arborea 8` and `forma arborea 4`, give
// three DIFFERENT bit patterns, and each is exactly the bits §5.4's
// group-and-fold definition gives. A shape that could not change the
// answer would not need declaring, and a language that let the machine
// pick it would be the ambient state §1 exists to remove. So this
// program computes all three in one process, over one array, and writes
// all three out; `expected.out` decides whether they are the RIGHT three.
//
// Measured, 2026-09-26, on tests/data/contractio_viginti.bin:
//
//   shape        scaled i64    the f32 result     its bits
//   ordinata      121209096    14.4492502...     0x41673021
//   arborea 8     121209080    14.4492483...     0x4167301F
//   arborea 4     121209088    14.4492493...     0x41673020
//
// Three distinct patterns, two ulps apart end to end: `arborea 4` sits
// one ulp below `ordinata` and `arborea 8` one ulp below that. Nothing
// about the VALUES is special -- twenty ordinary dyadic rationals,
// fourteen of them between 0.037 and 1.77 and six of a few 2^-23 units,
// four of the twenty negative -- and nothing about the sum is near an
// edge: no infinity, no NaN, no subnormal, no cancellation. The shapes
// disagree because float addition is not associative, which is the
// whole of §5.4.
//
// THE GOLDEN IS NOT OURS. expected.out is produced by
// prototypes/contractio_oracle.py, which implements §5.4's paragraph in
// Python from the paragraph and nothing of this compiler -- the
// "not our own bug" check, the way tests/programs/pictura_triangulum/
// uses prototypes/pictura_oracle.py. If the compiler and the oracle
// agree byte for byte, two independent readings of §5.4 landed on the
// same bits; if they disagree, one of them misread the section, and
// which one is a question with an answer.
//
// THE WIRE FORMAT is the oracle's docstring, and this file is the other
// half of it:
//
//   stdin   twenty values, each a little-endian signed 32-bit integer n
//           with |n| < 2^24, meaning the `f32` value n / 8388608.0
//           (2^-23 scaling: exact, a power of two, so no rounding
//           happens on the way in and the input is the input).
//   stdout  three little-endian signed 64-bit integers,
//           ordinata / arborea 8 / arborea 4, each ftoi(r * 8388608.0).
//           Exact for every r with |r| >= 1 and |r| < 2^40: r's ulp is
//           then at least 2^-23, so r * 2^23 is an integer and the
//           scaling is an exponent change. The fixed input's r is
//           about 14.45, whose ulp is 2^-20, so each result is an exact
//           multiple of 8 -- 121209096 and its two neighbours below.
//
// An integer wire rather than raw f32 bytes is deliberate: `@transitus`
// has no float row this program could lean on, and a scaled integer is
// byte-order-explicit at the one place it matters (the `deorsum` /
// `sicut u8` extraction below), which is what makes the same golden the
// right answer on a big-endian host.
//
// WHY THE INPUT IS A FILE AND NOT A LITERAL. An `acies` literal would
// make the twenty values part of the program, and then a mutation to the
// program could change the input along with the shape. Reading them from
// tests/data/contractio_viginti.bin -- the file the ORACLE generated --
// means the compiled program and the oracle are fed the identical bytes
// by the harness, and neither can move them.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED HERE: nothing about the C backend. It refuses this
// module (exit 4, `bfc: emitter: unsupported type`, the `red.F` handle
// type reaching library mode, which holds integers, addresses and
// floats and nothing else -- c-backend.md D4/D8), so the TEST directive
// carries `c-exsc-exit=4` and the differential phase checks the refusal
// rather than a second set of bits, and the directory is NOT `cross=yes`
// -- the cross phase goes through `--emitte c` too and would fail at the
// same refusal. §14 entry 28's two backend-parity clauses are
// unmeasured; see this directory's TEST file.

// scribe_octo -- one i64, little-endian, on the writer. Eight bytes, low
// significance first, extracted with `deorsum` and a narrowing cast:
// §5.4 makes the shift unsigned-only and same-type including the count
// (hence `sicut u64` on both sides), and §5.4's narrowing rule makes
// `sicut u8` the low eight bits of the two's-complement representation,
// never a trap -- which is exactly "write this byte". The count runs
// 0, 8 .. 56, all below 64, so the trap on a count at or above the
// width is never armed.
//
// `q sicut u64` is the equal-width signed-to-unsigned reinterpretation
// (§5.4): the same bits read as unsigned, so a negative result would
// come out as its two's-complement image, which IS its little-endian
// encoding. The fixed input's three results are all positive, so that
// path is exercised by none of them -- said here rather than implied.
//
// Private, so §4.1 rule 5 infers the capability row from the body:
// `s.scribe_octeto(b)` draws the writer's own mark, the shape
// tests/programs/lector_numerus/ writes.
functio scribe_octo(s: Scriptor, q: i64) -> mensura {
    firma u: u64 = q sicut u64;
    mutabilis n: mensura = 0;
    per k in 0..8 {
        firma sh: u64 = (k sicut u64) * 8;
        n = n + s.scribe_octeto((u deorsum sh) sicut u8);
    }
    redde n;
}

publica functio initium(m: Mundus) -> u8 {
    firma a = m.ambitus();
    sub ambitus = a;
    firma l = Lector.ab_introitu(a);
    firma s = Scriptor.ad_exitum(a);

    // The twenty values, decoded once. `mutabilis` because the
    // reductions below read it and nothing else writes it after this
    // loop; the repeat form zero-fills it (§8.6), as
    // examples/pictura/pictura.exsc's framebuffer does.
    mutabilis xs: acies<f32, 20> = [0.0; 20];

    // `defectus` rather than an early `redde`: the read loop must
    // consume its four bytes per value whatever it finds, so that a
    // short input is ONE verdict at the end and not a partial read
    // whose position depends on where the truncation was.
    // `lege_octeto` answers 256 at end of input and on an I/O error
    // alike (the prelude's documented hole, `eventus` being spec §11's
    // and not yet syntax), so this program cannot tell a truncated
    // input from a broken pipe -- both are exit 1, which is the honest
    // answer for a fixture whose input is a committed file.
    mutabilis defectus: u8 = 0;

    per i in 0..20 {
        // Little-endian, four bytes, then the sign. The bytes occupy
        // disjoint bit positions, so `+` and `*` on `i64` reassemble
        // the value with no overflow to trap on -- the largest term is
        // 255 * 16777216 and the largest total 2^32 - 1, and even the
        // 256 end-of-input sentinel, which makes the term 2^32 and the
        // total a little above it, is far inside `i64`. That is why no
        // bitwise word appears here: the arithmetic is exact, and `sive`
        // would say the same thing with one more contextual word in the
        // fixture.
        mutabilis n: i64 = 0;
        mutabilis scala: i64 = 1;
        per k in 0..4 {
            firma c: u16 = l.lege_octeto();
            si c ge 256 {
                defectus = 1;
            }
            n = n + (c sicut i64) * scala;
            scala = scala * 256;
        }
        // Unsigned 32-bit reading to signed. Done in `i64` arithmetic
        // rather than by an equal-width `sicut i32` reinterpretation so
        // that the decode is a value computation and not a claim about
        // the host's word: the same two statements give the same number
        // where `mensura` is 32 bits and where it is 64.
        si n ge 2147483648 {
            n = n - 4294967296;
        }
        // Both steps exact: |n| < 2^24 needs at most 24 significant
        // bits, so `sicut f32` (itof i64 -> f32; the refused direction
        // is u64 -> f32, ssa-ir.md 2.6) rounds nothing, and dividing by
        // 2^23 is an exponent change. The input reaches the reduction
        // unrounded, so every rounding this program performs belongs to
        // a reduction, which is what makes the three answers a
        // statement about the shapes.
        xs[i] = (n sicut f32) / 8388608.0;
    }
    si defectus ne 0 {
        redde 1;
    }

    // THE THREE REDUCTIONS. One array, three declared shapes, three
    // `contrahe` accumulators. `quisque` and not `per`: the iterations
    // do not observe one another, which is §8.5's independence claim and
    // the half of §5.5's bit-identity that `contrahe ... forma` does not
    // supply by itself. Writing to the accumulator IS the contribution
    // (§5.4); the body names it once and never reads it, and reading it
    // would be EXS-E0341. There is no `rumpe` here and could not be --
    // EXS-E0342.
    //
    // Each accumulator's element type F is the first contribution's
    // right-hand type, `f32` from `xs[i]`; nothing annotates it. After
    // the loop it is an ordinary immutable binding of F, which is what
    // the writes below read.
    quisque i in 0..20
        contrahe so: +
        forma ordinata
    {
        so = xs[i];
    }

    // `arborea 8` over twenty values is groups [0..8) [8..16) [16..20) --
    // §5.4's own worked example, remainder group included, and the
    // reason twenty is the count.
    quisque i in 0..20
        contrahe s8: +
        forma arborea 8
    {
        s8 = xs[i];
    }

    // `arborea 4` divides twenty exactly: five full groups, no
    // remainder. Beside `arborea 8` it separates "the width matters"
    // from "the remainder rule matters" -- two claims that one shape
    // could not tell apart.
    quisque i in 0..20
        contrahe s4: +
        forma arborea 4
    {
        s4 = xs[i];
    }

    // ftoi truncates toward zero inside `sicut i64`; each product is an
    // exact integer-valued f32 (see the header), so truncation has
    // nothing to remove and the byte-identity with the oracle does not
    // rest on a rounding mode.
    mutabilis w: mensura = 0;
    w = w + scribe_octo(s, (so * 8388608.0) sicut i64);
    w = w + scribe_octo(s, (s8 * 8388608.0) sicut i64);
    w = w + scribe_octo(s, (s4 * 8388608.0) sicut i64);
    si w ne 24 {
        redde 2;
    }
    redde 0;
}
